econdary sites. Examples: [pituitary gland adenoma](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0006373), [breast adenoma](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0002058)*\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:145:Examples: [adult brain stem neoplasm](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0024797), [adult-onset myasthenia gravis](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018324)*\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:312:*This is a design pattern for classes representing benign neoplasms, extending a generic neoplasm class. For example, a benign adrenal gland pheochromocytoma, defined as being the benign form of the more general adrenal gland pheochromocytoma.\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:320:| Variables | neoplasm (MONDO:0005070),  |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:325:| defined:class                                | defined:class:label                   | neoplasm                                     | neoplasm:label                                              |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:328:| MONDO:0020581 | benign PEComa                         | MONDO:0006359 | neoplasm with perivascular epithelioid cell differentiation |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:330:| MONDO:0036781 | benign axillary neoplasm              | MONDO:0036779 | axillary neoplasm                                           |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:334:### Benign neoplasm\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:336:This is a design pattern for classes representing *benign* neoplasms based on their location.\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:341:| IRI | http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/patterns/benign_neoplasm.yaml |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:342:| Name | benign_neoplasm |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:346:| Examples | [mondo](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/blob/master/src/patterns/data/matches/benign_neoplasm.tsv) |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:351:| MONDO:0002193 | Bartholin gland benign neoplasm  | UBERON:0000460 | major vestibular gland                     |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:352:| MONDO:0044764 | benign choroid plexus neoplasm   | UBERON:0001886 | choroid plexus                             |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:353:| MONDO:0002278 | benign colon neoplasm            | UBERON:0001155 | colon                                      |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:354:| MONDO:0006105 | benign conjunctival neoplasm     | UBERON:0001811 | conjunctiva                                |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:355:| MONDO:0000385 | benign digestive system neoplasm | UBERON:0005409 | alimentary part of gastrointestinal system |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:357:See full table [here](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/blob/master/src/patterns/data/matches/benign_neoplasm.tsv)\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:359:*Cancers are malignant neoplasms arising from a variety of different cell types.\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:383:*Carcinomas are malignant neoplasms arising from epithelial cells.\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:733:*A hemangioma (a benign vascular lesion characterized by the formation of capillary-sized or cavernous vascular channels) that is located in a specific anatomical site.\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1030:*A leiomyoma (a well-circumscribed benign smooth muscle neoplasm characterized by the presence of spindle cells with cigar-shaped nuclei, interlacing fascicles, and a whorled pattern) that is located in a specific anatomical entity.\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1053:*An uncommon, aggressive malignant smooth muscle neoplasm, usually occurring in post-menopausal women that is characterized by a proliferation of neoplastic spindle cells that is located in a specific anatomical location.\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1076:*This pattern is applied to diseases that are described as having a lesion, where a localized pathological or traumatic structural change, damage, deformity, or discontinuity of tissue, organ, or body part is present.\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1077:Examples: [ulnar nerve lesion](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0001458 ), [peripheral nerve lesion](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0024334)*\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1081:| IRI | http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/patterns/lesioned.yaml |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1082:| Name | lesioned |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1180:*This is a design pattern for classes representing malignant neoplasms, extending a generic neoplasm class.\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1188:| Variables | neoplasm (owl:Thing),  |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1193:| defined:class                                | defined:class:label           | neoplasm                                     | neoplasm:label               |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1195:| MONDO:0004992 | cancer                        | MONDO:0005070 | neoplasm                     |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1326:This is a design pattern for classes representing neoplasms based on their location. This may be the site of origin, but it can also represent a secondary site for malignant neoplasms that have metastasized.\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1328:Note that tumor is typically a synonym for neoplasm, although this can be context dependent. For NETs, NCIT uses the nomenclature 'tumor' to indicate 'well differentiated, low or intermediate grade tumor'. This can also be called carcinoid, see https://www.cancer.org/cancer/gastrointestinal-carcinoid-tumor/about/what-is-gastrointestinal-carcinoid.html We attempt to spell this out in our labels.*\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1332:| IRI | http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/patterns/neoplasm.yaml |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1333:| Name | neoplasm |\nsrc/patterns/dosdp-patterns/README.md:1337:| Examples | [mondo]